  "query_preview": "..\nFriday night through Monday: The Inland Northwest is currently under \na deep southerly flow as a large upper-level low and occluded front \napproaches the northwest coast. This low will weaken as it moves \ninland and the base of the trough dives into northern California. \nWeak synoptic ascent associated with the occluded front will \ngenerate a band of light precipitation tonight into Saturday \nmorning. Precipitation totals will be maximized over the Cascades, \nPalouse, Camas Prairie, and the central Idaho Panhandle with amounts \nup to 0.40 inches. \n\nClearing in the wake of this precipitation will promote low stratus \nand fog development early Saturday morning, particularly within \nsheltered valleys, the Palouse, and the Spokane area.  Weather dries \nout briefly during the day on Saturday with afternoon temperatures \ncooling by 5-10 degrees. A post-frontal cold pool aloft (-20C to -\n23C) will steepen mid-level lapse rates, supporting isolated light \nshowers across the Cascades, far northeast Washington, and north \nIdaho. Winds will be breezy out of the south Saturday afternoon with \ngusts 15-25 mph across the Basin into the Spokane area and Palouse. \n\nActive weather continues late Saturday night into Sunday with \nanother frontal system approaching the northwest coast. The leading \nwarm front and surface south to southeast flow will support light \nrain across the east slopes of the Cascades and the western Columbia \nBasin early Sunday morning before the trailing cold front brings a \nreturn of downsloping flow off the Cascades by the mid to late \nmorning. Stronger jet dynamics associated with this front will \nprovide better forcing to the Inland Northwest as it moves into far \neastern Washington and north Idaho on Sunday. The front will slow \ndown through the day, prolonging precipitation over eastern \nWashington and north Idaho through early Monday morning and \nresulting in higher precipitation totals than the Friday system. NBM \nrainfall amounts show up to 0.15 inches in the immediate lee of the \nCascades and 0.10 to 0.60 inches across the Cascades, far eastern \nWashington and north Idaho. Snow levels across the Cascades, \nnortheast Washington and far north Idaho will be around 5000 to 5500 \nfeet Saturday night through Sunday afternoon with snow amounts 2 to \n5 inches above pass level and 1 to 4 inches at Washington and \nSherman Pass level. \n\nTuesday through Thursday: A brief lull in the active weather is \nexpected on Tuesday though it will be chilly. Light winds and \nresidual boundary layer moisture will favor fog and stratus \ndevelopment across much of the region.\n\nConfidence in the forecast decreases for the Wednesday and Thursday \ntimeframe as the next upper-level low approaches the region. While \nmodels are in solid agreement on the presence of this system and for \nit to move inland, there remains solid differences on the exact \ntrack. Around 51% of global ensembles show the center of this low \ndiving into Oregon and northern California, leaving the Inland \nNorthwest on the drier side of this low. The other 49% have the \ntrack farther north across Washington and Oregon, which would be the \nscenario to monitor for mountain pass snow and lowland snow/rain. \n/vmt",
